The deepest stack comes from evaluating `combined.check_update(update)` on the XOR-composed filter tree in `telegram/ext/filters.py`. `BaseFilter.__xor__` builds an `_XORFilter`, and `_XORFilter.__init__` expands XOR into `(base_filter & ~xor_filter) | (~base_filter & xor_filter)`, creating several nested `_MergedFilter` and `_InvertedFilter` objects. At runtime, `UpdateFilter.check_update` calls `_XORFilter.filter`, which calls `merged_filter.check_update`; from there the longest path is a chain of nested `_MergedFilter.filter` calls down to a `MessageFilter.check_update`, which itself calls `BaseFilter.check_update`, `Update.effective_message`, and then a concrete filter like `Regex.filter` or `CaptionRegex.filter`. Following that longest nested call path through the composite structure gives a maximum simultaneous call depth of 15.

